<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <!--
      A transitive dependency pinned by a direct <PackageReference> without a version here should be
      an error rather than a silent floating restore.
    -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="MCP">
    <!--
      The transport. 2.2.0 is what Boltway.Mcp 0.2.0 and later depend on at >= 2.2.0, so pinning
      anything lower here is NU1605 the moment step 8 of docs/DESIGN.md §7 adds that reference - a
      package downgrade, which is an error in this repository rather than a warning. Another consumer
      found that out by restoring rather than by reading: these two versions are one decision, and
      the one written down here is the one that has to move first.

      2.2.0 is also where `WithRequestFilters` / `AddCallToolFilter` / `AddListToolsFilter` live,
      which is the single place docs/DESIGN.md §6.5.5 puts the per-tool gate. Step 2 depends on it.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore" Version="2.2.0" />

    <!-- Pinned exactly rather than to a range. docs/DESIGN.md §6.2, and the reason is the same
         one Boltway's own VERSIONING.md gives: a published version is frozen forever, so naming one
         is naming a tree somebody can go and read.

         0.4.0, up from 0.3.0, for two things this repository stopped hand-writing at that version:
         AddJwksSigningKeys moved into Boltway.ResourceServer, where a resource server looks for it,
         and Boltway.ResourceServer.Testing appeared. 0.3.0 remains the release that first carried
         CallerPrincipal.ScopeClaim, which is what makes an empty scope set stop meaning three
         different things - nothing here needs a version older than that. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Boltway.ResourceServer" Version="0.4.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Boltway.OAuth.Net" Version="0.4.0" />

    <!-- Test-only, and referenced from both OAuth suites rather than only the Boltway one.

         What it carries is RFC 9728 conformance run against a wired pipeline - eight assertions
         over an HttpClient, a resource identifier and one protected path, with no Boltway type in
         its surface. That makes it as true of this repository's own OAuth mode as of the Boltway
         one, and the defect it exists to catch (a host whose authentication middleware does not
         read the framework's anonymous marker, so the discovery document answers 401) is a defect
         SshWarden.OAuth can have just as easily.

         **It is a test dependency and it stays one.** src/SshWarden.OAuth references nothing from
         Boltway and must not begin to: a deployment on any other authorization server is the case
         that assembly exists for. Deriving a conformance suite is not the same as depending on the
         implementation it was written beside. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Boltway.ResourceServer.Testing" Version="0.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Configuration">
    <!--
      TOML, and not appsettings.json, because of what docs/DESIGN.md §6.4 asks of this file: "reading
      the config file tells you immediately who can touch what, without having to work it out". The
      grant table in §6.5.4 is an array of tables - one block per rule, each naming a subject, the
      scopes it needs, the tools it covers and the hosts it reaches. TOML writes that as repeated
      [[grant]] blocks; JSON writes it as an array of brace-nested objects that a reviewer has to
      count commas through. The file is a security surface read by a human under time pressure, so
      the format is chosen for that reader.

      Tomlyn is BSD-2-Clause, and its parse failure carries every diagnostic with a line and column
      rather than only the first - which is what lets the loader's refusal name the line it refused
      instead of asking the operator to find it (docs/DESIGN.md's "a refusal names its boundary",
      inherited from Boltway's CLAUDE.md). Measured against 2.10.1 on 2026-08-26: a truncated table
      header reports `(1,8) : error : Expected `]` but was ...`.

      **What to know before moving off this version.** 2.x targets TOML 1.1.0 and its own readme says
      it does not support 1.0. TOML 1.1 is not a finalised revision, so that is a dependency on a
      moving target - accepted here because 1.1 only adds syntax to 1.0, so a file written to the
      finalised revision (which is all the example config and everything the README documents uses)
      parses under either, and the risk is one-directional: a config using 1.1-only syntax would not
      be readable by other tooling. Do not reach for 1.1-only syntax in anything this repository
      ships. The 0.x line of this package is the TOML 1.0 parser with a different API, if that
      trade ever needs reversing.
    -->
    <!--
      For the log declarations in SshWarden/Diagnostics. Abstractions only: this project holds the
      security decisions and is meant to be testable without a host, so it takes the interface and
      leaves every provider to whoever composes the process.
    -->
    <!--
      The generic OAuth path. The framework's own bearer handler rather than a hand-written
      validator: it does OpenID Connect discovery, key rotation and the RFC 9068 checks, and every
      .NET reader already knows what it is.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Tomlyn" Version="2.10.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="SSH">
    <!--
      MIT, and the semantics docs/DESIGN.md §4.1 wants are already here rather than something to build:
      a client that owns the TCP connection and the SSH handshake, and a new channel per command.
      What matters is what is *not* used - CreateShellStream keeps shell state across commands,
      which is the thing this project deliberately does not have, and the library's own forum
      advice recommends it for running several commands. It is the main trap at this layer.

      Two behaviours measured against 2026.0.0's own XML docs on 2026-08-26, because both shape the
      code: ConnectionInfo.MaxSessions defaults to 10, so one client per host is a ceiling of ten
      concurrent commands whatever any other limit says; and CancelAsync documents that "when the
      server does not implement signals, it may send no response", which is why a timeout is
      wrapped on the remote side rather than trusted to cancellation.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SSH.NET" Version="2026.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Tests">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.9.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.0" />
    <!--
      An in-memory server, so the authentication middleware is exercised through a real HTTP
      pipeline - routing, endpoint metadata, headers and status codes - without binding a port. The
      thing under test is what a caller gets back, and asserting on middleware in isolation would
      not have caught the ordering mistake that makes the endpoint-metadata opt-out invisible.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
